From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 16:23:49 -0400
Subject: spaces: file tabs on open, follow the switch, never orphan a tab

Spaces had a switcher, colours and persistence, and every capture looked
right, while the model underneath decided membership lazily: a tab joined
a Space the first time something asked, which in practice was when a
context menu opened. A tab opened in Space B showed in every Space, a
switch left the content pane on the old Space's page with nothing
selected in the sidebar, and deleting a Space hid its tabs until the next
restart. None of that is visible in a screenshot, which is why it lasted.

The model now observes the tab strip. A tab is filed under the active
Space (or the Space it was restored from) the moment it is inserted, and
forgotten when it leaves, which retires ForgetClosedTabs. Switching Spaces
settles the active tab before observers hide anything: the tab the user
last had active there, else the first tab in the Space, else an essentials
tab, else a new tab opened in the Space. Closing the last visible tab of a
Space opens a replacement one turn later, since the strip is mid-notification
when it happens. Deleting a Space moves its tabs into the active Space.
Moving a tab between Spaces notifies observers, and the visibility
controller now watches the strip too, so restored and moved tabs are hidden
or shown without waiting for a switch.

docs/features/spaces.md names these as behaviours B1-B8; each has a test
in space_model_window_unittest.cc against a real Browser and TabStripModel.

Why: a Space whose membership depends on when a menu was opened is a
picture of a feature, and the mandate says features ship complete.
Removable when: the tab collection tree grows a first-class notion of tab
sets and Spaces become a collection type (ADR 0015, option C).
